From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PS3 Linux project <ps3@localhost>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 16:54:13 +0300
Subject: CPU skinning through libspeq on the PS3's SPEs

With GPU skinning off (the PS3 default, 0002), every animated skeletal mesh group is skinned on the CPU each frame: interpverts moves every vertex by its bone or blended dual quaternion into vdata, which is then uploaded. On the PS3 that is PPE time the game's frame waits on. libspeq (ps3-spu) does the same transform on the Cell's SPEs, or with its own vector code on the PPE, into a buffer of its own that is uploaded instead.

libspeq is loaded at run time with dlopen: the engine doesn't depend on it, and without it, when it declines (SPEQ_SKIN=off, a mesh group under SPEQ_SKIN_MIN vertices, the SPEs set aside after failures), when it is late (its timeout) or with speqskin 0, the engine skins as before. A mesh group libspeq can't take (a palette over 1536 entries) is remembered and left to the engine. vdata is the template of libspeq's output, so the texture coordinates come along. All policy (which unit, sizes, timeouts, SPEQ_SKIN=auto|spu|ppe|off, SPEQ_DEBUG) lives in libspeq.

Forwarded: no

